# The tool coverage each shimmed hook's registration must declare.
#
# NOTHING VERIFIES THIS FILE, AS OF 2026-08-04. The axis that read it was part of
# the conformance instrument retired that day, and it was not rebuilt: the check
# needed to parse settings.json, and rebuilding it is how the instrument grew to
# six times the size of the contract it measured the first time. This file is a
# declaration a person reads when wiring a project, and the requirement it serves
# ("Registration matches the implementation's tool coverage") is stated and not
# machine-checked. That is a deliberate trade, not an oversight — if a
# registration drifts, it will be found by the hook not firing, which is how the
# defect below was found in the first place.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS.
#
# Nothing read matchers at fleet scope. The conformance tool opened
# settings.json only to enumerate override env vectors, so a registration could
# be narrower than the implementation it invokes and every axis still reported
# the project current: the marker matched, the shim was byte-identical, and the
# hook simply never fired for the tools nobody had registered.
#
# That is not hypothetical. Five repositories registered database-sentinel on
# `Bash|Edit|Write` while the reconciled implementation declared
# `Bash|Edit|Write|MultiEdit`, so a MultiEdit to .env did not invoke the hook at
# all — protection absent rather than degraded, and invisible to every check.
#
# WHY A DECLARATION RATHER THAN A READ OF THE IMPLEMENTATION.
#
# The implementation's coverage is what its payload tests accept, which cannot
# be read out of the file without executing it or parsing its conditionals. The
# expected set is therefore declared, exactly as ARTIFACTS, SHIMMED-HOOKS and
# FLEET are — an expected set discovered from what you happened to find cannot
# detect a missing member.
#
# WHAT A MISMATCH MEANS, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS.
#
# A registration NARROWER than this declaration is a coverage gap: the hook does
# not fire where it is meant to. A registration WIDER is not automatically a
# defect — a project may guard more than the fleet requires — so it is reported
# as wider and not as a failure. Both are reported; neither is silently passed.
#
# AND A HOOK REGISTERED NOWHERE IS THE THIRD DIRECTION, which the first revision
# of this axis missed. A hook no settings entry names has no registration to be
# narrow — the comparison loop simply had nothing to iterate, printed nothing,
# and a shim that was current, byte-identical and wired to nothing scored clean
# on every axis. That is the worse case by this file's own reasoning: protection
# ABSENT rather than degraded, reported as neither.
#
# It is reachable by exactly the edit a contract rollout performs. The 1.2.0
# propagation rewrites settings.json in five repositories, and dropping an entry
# is the plausible mistake there — so the axis added to verify that rollout was
# blind to the way the rollout most plausibly fails.
#
# A hook a project has DECLARED it does not bind (see OPT-OUTS) is exempt: no
# registration is the correct state for it, and reporting it would leave the
# declaration meaning nothing here.
#
# Format: <hook> <event> <matcher>. One per line, blank lines and # ignored.

database-sentinel     PreToolUse   Bash|Edit|Write|MultiEdit
openspec-change-gate  PreToolUse   Edit|Write|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit
normalize-claude-md   PostToolUse  Edit|Write|MultiEdit
